Now audit for these categories ONLY:

A) Composer file missing
- Detect Composer usage (autoload.php, vendor/ usage, composer install references) but missing composer.json in plugin root.
- Finding criteria: Using composer but no /composer.json (or missing in expected plugin folder).

B) No publicly documented source for compiled/minified JS/CSS
- Find minified/webpack bundles in build/ or dist/ (e.g., “webpackBootstrap”, huge single-line files, *.min.js, *.min.css).
- Verify presence of non-compiled source (src/) OR a public link + build instructions in readme.txt.
- Flag if compiled files exist without source and without readme documentation.

C) Calling core loading files directly
- Find includes/requires of: wp-load.php, wp-config.php, wp-blog-header.php
- Also flag includes of wp-admin core files when used improperly or for direct execution.
- Provide WP-approved alternatives: hooks, REST, AJAX, rewrite/query_vars.

D) Incorrect file/directory location handling
- Flag hardcoded WP paths or WP_CONTENT_DIR-based assumptions and manual concatenation of plugin paths.
- Require plugin_dir_path, plugin_dir_url, plugins_url, wp_upload_dir.

E) Unsafe SQL calls
- Flag: mysqli/new mysqli, PDO to WP DB, raw queries without prepare, interpolated variables in SQL, unprepared IN() or LIMIT/OFFSET, direct table name interpolation.
- Require: $wpdb, $wpdb->prepare(), strict casting, placeholder usage.

F) Internationalization mismatch (text domain vs plugin slug)
- Check all gettext calls: __(), _e(), esc_html__(), esc_attr__(), etc.
- Verify text domain matches EXACT plugin slug provided above.
- If mismatch exists, list occurrences and recommend a consistent text domain strategy.

G) Processing whole input
- Flag reading entire php://input without strict need; assigning $_REQUEST wholesale; iterating full $_POST/$_GET/$_REQUEST.
- Require: read only needed keys and sanitize/validate each.

H) Output escaping missing (“escape late”)
- Flag echo/print of variables/options/untrusted data without correct escaping.
- Include context-specific escaping recommendations (esc_html, esc_attr, esc_url, wp_kses_post).
- Flag risky patterns like echoing XML/JSON directly in admin screens.

I) Generic / conflicting names (functions/classes/constants/namespaces/options/transients)
- Find short prefixes (<4 chars) like WA_, generic names, wp_ or _ prefix misuse.
- Flag unprefixed options/transients (set_option, update_option, set_transient).
- Require unique prefix across plugin. Provide rename map suggestions.

J) Stored XSS risk from imported sheet links (or similar untrusted URLs)
- Find any feature that imports URLs from external sources (Google Sheets, CSV, API) and renders them.
- Flag if URLs are not sanitized on save (esc_url_raw) and not escaped on output (esc_url), or if HTML is rendered without wp_kses.
- Provide concrete hardening steps.

K) Allowing direct file access
- Find PHP files under templates/, admin/, includes/ that execute code when accessed directly.
- Flag missing:
  if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) exit;

L) Out-of-date libraries
- Detect embedded libraries with version banners (e.g., Chart.js).
- Flag if version is old and recommend upgrade to latest stable and where to track it.

M) Combining/renaming JS files in a way that breaks WP core script usage
- Flag bundling core libraries or re-shipping them; enqueuing own renamed versions of core libs.
- Require dependency-based enqueues using wp_enqueue_script.

N) Using CURL instead of WP HTTP API
- Find curl_init/curl_exec usage in plugin code (not vendor).
- Require wp_remote_get/post and error handling.

O) Not using wp_enqueue_* properly
- Flag echoing <script> tags, directly linking /wp-includes/ paths, printing <link> tags.
- Require admin_enqueue_scripts / wp_enqueue_scripts + wp_add_inline_script/style.

P) Setting default timezone
- Find date_default_timezone_set calls.
- Require WP timezone-safe functions (wp_date, current_time).

Q) Including libraries already in core / Remote asset loading
- Flag loading jQuery externally (code.jquery.com) or other remote JS/CSS like cdn.datatables.net.
- Require local bundling or WP-registered scripts.

R) Trademark risks in display name/readme
- Scan readme.txt and main plugin headers for display name beginning with trademarked terms (example: “Google …”).
- Flag likely trademark-leading names and suggest compliant alternatives.

S) Incorrect stable tag
- Compare readme Stable tag vs main plugin Version header.
- Flag mismatch and suggest SemVer + tag practices.

T) Changing active plugins
- Flag activate_plugin/deactivate_plugins, Plugin_Upgrader usage that changes other plugins without user action.
- Suggest dependency notices and safe failure behavior.

U) Unclosed ob_start
- Find ob_start() calls.
- Ensure every ob_start has guaranteed closure in same logical flow.

V) Nonces + permission checks (and nonce bypass)
- Find privileged actions triggered by GET/POST/REQUEST, AJAX, REST, admin-post.
- Flag missing nonces or capability checks.
- Flag “optional nonce” logic (nonce checked only if other conditions true).
- Provide corrected “fail closed” patterns.

W) Keyword stuffing
- Scan readme.txt sections, short description, tags, and headings for repetitive unnatural keyword lists.
- Flag suspicious repetition and recommend cleanup.

X) Arbitrary custom CSS/JS/PHP insertion (“script/code injection” features)
- Find any UI/settings/API that lets users save or render arbitrary CSS, JS, or PHP.
  Examples: “Custom CSS” textarea, “Custom JS”, “Header scripts”, “Footer scripts”, “Custom PHP”, template editors.
- Flag if:
  - The plugin stores raw code in options/meta and outputs it on frontend or admin
  - The plugin allows arbitrary JS injection or executes user-provided PHP
- For findings, show:
  - Where the code is accepted (settings field / REST / AJAX)
  - Where it is stored (option name / meta key)
  - Where it is output/executed (hooks/templates)
  - Why it violates WP.org guidelines (arbitrary script insertion risk)
  - A compliant redesign: replace with structured settings (inputs/toggles), generate safe output programmatically
- Note: Do NOT recommend “sanitize with wp_kses” as a solution for JavaScript execution. Treat arbitrary JS/PHP as disallowed.

Y) “Powered by” / Credit links shown on frontend without explicit opt-in
- Scan all frontend output (shortcodes, blocks, widgets, templates, React/Vue components, footer renderers) for attribution:
  “Powered by”, “Built with”, credit backlinks, branded footer links, etc.
- Flag if attribution appears by default or without clear admin opt-in.
- Allowed: admin-only pages (plugin settings), code-level license attribution.
- For each finding, provide:
  - Output location + snippet
  - Whether it is frontend vs admin
  - The fix: default OFF + explicit checkbox opt-in (with clear label) + store preference in a prefixed option.

Deliverable format:
1) Start with a short “What I scanned” section (folders/files).
2) Then list findings grouped by category (A–W), but ONLY include categories where you found issues.
3) Under each category, list findings as numbered items.
4) End with a “Top blockers before WP.org submission” list (max 10), pulled from your findings.

